tel 1.0

base gossamer
import soundness

info   text as a typed value: `Text`, the `t"…"` interpolator, and a vocabulary of operations over any textual type
detail
    _Gossamer_ is the text library: the operations on `Text` — the opaque alias of the JVM's
    `String` which Soundness uses in place of `String` everywhere — and the typeclasses which
    make those same operations available on any other textual type, such as an ANSI-styled
    `Teletype`, an `Ascii` byte string or a `Writing` of grapheme clusters.

    A `Text` is written with the `t"…"` interpolator, which substitutes any value that is
    `Showable` and processes escapes while the code compiles, or with `txt"…"`, which also
    folds a paragraph written across several source lines into one. Its operations are plain
    verbs: `cut` divides at a delimiter and `join` reassembles; `keep`, `skip`, `snip`,
    `before`, `after`, `upto`, `from` and `segment` slice by count or by ordinal position;
    `lower`, `upper`, `capitalize`, `trim`, `pad`, `fit` and `sub` transform; `starts`, `ends`,
    `contains`, `offsetOf`, `tally`, `pinpoint` and `search` ask questions. Absence is an
    `Optional`, not `-1` or `null`; positions are `Ordinal`s (`Prim`, `Sec`, `Ter`, …), which
    cannot be confused with lengths; and a `cut` is proved to yield at least one piece.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import textMetrics.uniformMetric

    val name = t"Nick Bottom"
    name.words.map(_.keep(1)).join            // t"NB"
    name.cut(t" ").head.upper                 // t"NICK"
    name.offsetOf(t"Bott")                    // an Optional ordinal, present here
    name.pad(15, Rtl, '.')                    // t"....Nick Bottom"
    t"one,two".cut(t",").join(t"(", t", ", t")")   // t"(one, two)"
    ```

    Most of the vocabulary is written once, over the `Textual` typeclass, so a type with a
    `Textual` instance — `Text`, `Ascii`, `Writing`, and escapade's `Teletype` — gets every
    operation without redefining any. `Textual` extends the general collection typeclasses
    (`Countable`, `Segmentable`), which is how the count-based and predicate-based `keep`,
    `skip` and `snip`, the ordinal-based `before`/`after`/`upto`/`from`, and `count`, `where`,
    `all` and `exists` from the collections library serve text too: those live in `rudiments`
    and `murmuration`, not here. The factories `reversibleTextual` and `traversableTextual`
    let a new textual type publish `reverse` and traversal in its own companion.

    Three things about text are policies, chosen by importing a given **by name** (because
    `import soundness.*` does not import givens):

    - **Case sensitivity** (`caseSensitivity.caseSensitive`, `caseInsensitive` or
      `smartCase`), which every proximity measure consults.
    - **Collation** (`collations.unicodeCollation` or `codepointCollation`), without which
      text cannot be sorted or compared with `<`: with `collationComparable`, a `Collation`
      in scope makes `Text` `Comparable`.
    - **Proximity** (`proximities.levenshteinProximity`, `jaroProximity`, …), the measure
      behind `proximity`, `fuzzy` and the `Lexicon`.

    `Decimalizer` renders a `Double` to a chosen number of significant figures or decimal
    places, and is what `show` on a `Double` consults; `decimalConverters.javaDecimalConverter`
    is the plain `toString` alternative.

    The library has three components:

    - `gossamer.core` — `Text`'s operations, `t"…"`, `txt"…"` and `a"…"`, `Textual`,
      `Builder` and `Text.build`, `Cuttable` and `Joinable`, `Writing` and `Grapheme`, `Ascii`,
      `Decimalizer`, `Numerous`, `CaseSensitivity`, `Collation`, `Proximity` and the choice
      packages `caseSensitivity`, `collations`, `proximities` and `decimalConverters`.
    - `gossamer.codec` — the `distillate`-facing half: `textDecodable`, which decodes bytes
      to `Text` through a `Charset`, and the `enumIdentification` package, which names enum
      cases in kebab, snake, camel or Pascal case. Split out so that a module which only
      needs text does not depend on `distillate`.
    - `gossamer.lexicon` — `Lexicon`, a BK-tree for similarity search over a vocabulary,
      and `Dictionary`, a dense trie for exact and prefix lookup.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella, and the choice packages merge with
    the umbrella's own, so a user writes `import soundness.*` and then imports one given of
    each policy by name.

refer anticipation.Text
refer gossamer.Textual
refer gossamer.t
refer gossamer.cut
refer gossamer.join
refer gossamer.Collation
refer gossamer.Proximity
refer gossamer.Decimalizer

entry .t   the `t"…"` interpolator: a `Text` from a literal, with `Showable` substitutions; and a `t"…"` pattern
  detail
      `t"Hello, $name!"` builds a `Text`. Each literal part is escape-processed while the
      code compiles (`\n`, `\t`, `→`, `\\`, `\$`; a bad escape is a compile error at the
      offending character), and each substitution is rendered through the `Showable` instance
      for its type — a value with no `Showable` is a compile error, _a value of `T` is not
      Showable_, rather than a silent `toString`. The result is `Text`, never `String`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val name = t"Hamlet"
      val count = 3
      t"Hello, $name! You have $count messages."   // t"Hello, Hamlet! You have 3 messages."
      t"tab\there"                                  // a real tab between the words
      ```

      The same prefix is a pattern: `case t"quit" =>` matches a `Text` equal to the literal,
      through `SimpleTExtractor`. The pattern form takes no substitutions. `fuzzy` accepts
      `t"…"` patterns in its cases, too.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def respond(command: Text): Text = command match
        case t"quit" => t"goodbye"
        case other   => t"unknown command: $other"
      ```

      `txt"…"` is the variant for multi-line prose, and `a"…"` makes an `Ascii`.
  refer gossamer.txt
  refer gossamer.SimpleTExtractor
  refer gossamer.fuzzy

entry .txt   the `txt"…"` interpolator: a `Text` with wrapped source lines folded into one
    As `t"…"`, with the whitespace of a literal written across several source lines
    normalised: every run of whitespace (including a single newline and the indentation after
    it) becomes one space, and a blank line — two newlines — becomes a single `\n`, so a
    paragraph wrapped for the source's reader is unwrapped for the output's, and a blank line
    separates paragraphs.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    txt"""This message is written
          across several lines."""      // t"This message is written across several lines."

    txt"""Hello
          world

          Bonjour
          le monde"""                   // t"Hello world\nBonjour le monde"
    ```

    Substitutions are rendered through `Showable`, as in `t"…"`, and are normalised with the
    rest of the text.

entry .a   the `a"…"` interpolator: an `Ascii` byte string, checked to be ASCII as the code compiles
    `a"hello"` is an `Ascii`, a frozen array of bytes holding the literal's characters, one byte
    each. A character outside ASCII is a compile error (number 824, _`é` is not a valid ASCII
    character_) reported at that character's own position in the source, not at the start of
    the literal. Substitutions are themselves `Ascii` values, concatenated in.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val name = a"world"
    a"hello $name".bytes.length   // 11
    a"hello café"                 // does not compile
    ```

entry .SimpleTExtractor   the extractor behind a `t"…"` pattern: equality with the literal
  detail
      `case t"quit" =>` desugars to a `SimpleTExtractor(t"quit")` whose `#unapply` is equality
      with the scrutinee. It exists so that `t"…"` can be written in a pattern as well as an
      expression; there is rarely a reason to construct one directly.
  entry #text   the literal the pattern matches
  entry #unapply   whether the scrutinee equals `#text`

entry .Textual   the typeclass which makes a type textual: `Text`, `Ascii`, `Writing`, `Teletype`
  detail
      `textual is Textual` says that `textual` is a sequence of characters with a known length,
      which can be sliced, mapped, searched and concatenated, built up in a `Builder`, and
      rendered as plain `Text`. Every generic operation in gossamer — `cut`, `join`, `words`,
      `lower`, `trim`, `pad`, `capitalize`, `uncamel`, `pinpoint`, `chomp`, … — is an
      extension on a receiver with a `Textual` instance, so a new textual type gains the whole
      vocabulary by providing one instance in its companion.

      The `#Result` type member is the element type: `Char` for `Text`, `Byte` for `Ascii`,
      `Grapheme` for `Writing`. Operations which act on characters (`lower`, `upper`, `trim`,
      `capitalize`, `tr`, `pad`, `pinpoint`, …) require `Textual { type Result = Char }`;
      those which act on positions and substrings (`cut`, `words`, `starts`, `offsetOf`,
      `slices`, `chomp`, …) take any `Textual`. `#Show[value]` is the typeclass a value must
      satisfy to be appended to a builder of this type (`Showable` for `Text`; escapade's
      `Teletype` uses its own, so a styled value appends with its styling).

      `Textual` extends `Countable` and `Segmentable` (with `Segment = Self` and `Operand =
      Ordinal`), which is what lets the general collection operations — `keep`, `skip`,
      `snip`, `before`, `after`, `upto`, `from`, `segment`, `count`, `where`, `all`, `exists` —
      serve textual values, and `Zeroic` and `Applicable`, so `textual.zero` is the empty
      value and `Textual[Teletype](text)` converts plain text in.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      def initials[textual: Textual { type Result = Char }](name: textual): textual =
        name.words.map(_.keep(1).upper).join

      initials(t"nick bottom")   // t"NB"
      ```

      The instances for `Text` (`.text`) and, through their companions, for `Ascii` and
      `Writing`, are in implicit scope. A type whose `reverse` and traversal should go through
      its `Textual` instance publishes `reversibleTextual` and `traversableTextual` in its own
      companion, since a blanket given could not be found from `rudiments`.
  refer gossamer.reversibleTextual
  refer gossamer.traversableTextual
  refer gossamer.Builder
  refer gossamer.Writing
  refer gossamer.Ascii
  entry .apply   `Textual[textual](text)`: converts a `Text` into any textual type
      The explicit form of `#apply`: `Textual[Teletype](t"plain")` wraps plain text as a styled
      value with no styling, and `Textual[Text](text)` is the identity.
  entry .text   the `Textual` instance for `Text` itself, with `Char` elements and `Showable` for `Show`
      Length, access, `indexOf`, `segment` and `map` go straight to the underlying `String`;
      the builder is a `TextBuilder`.
  entry .concatenable   `left + right` on any textual type, through `Textual#concat`
      The `Concatenable` given which gives every textual type `+`. It is a companion given of
      `Textual`, so it is found for any `textual: Textual`.
  entry #Result   the element type: `Char` for `Text`, `Byte` for `Ascii`, `Grapheme` for `Writing`
  entry #Show   the typeclass a value needs to be appended to a builder of this type
      `Show[value]` is `value is Showable` for `Text`, `Ascii` and `Writing`; a styled type
      may choose a richer one. `append` in a `Text.build` block requires it of anything which
      is neither a `Text` nor a `Char`.
  entry #Segment   the type a slice of this text has: the text's own type
  entry #Operand   the index type: `Ordinal`
  entry #show   renders a value as this textual type, through `#Show`
  entry #apply   converts a plain `Text` into this textual type
  entry #single   a one-element text from one `#Result`
  entry #fromChar   the `#Result` element corresponding to a `Char`
  entry #classTag   the `ClassTag` of the textual type, for allocating arrays of it
  entry #length   the number of elements: characters, bytes or graphemes
  entry #text   the plain `Text` of a value, with any styling dropped
  entry #map   a new text with a function applied to every element
  entry #empty   the empty value of this type
  entry #concat   two values joined into one
  entry #access   the element at an ordinal position; no check is made that it is in range
  entry #contains   whether an ordinal is a valid position in the text
  entry #indexOf   the position of a substring's first occurrence at or after `start`, or `Unset`
      The primitive behind `offsetOf`, `contains` and `tally`. The substring is always plain
      `Text`, whatever the textual type.
  entry #lastIndexOf   the position of a substring's last occurrence, or `Unset`
      By default a scan of repeated `#indexOf` calls; `Text`'s instance overrides it with the
      `String` primitive. An empty substring is `Unset`.
  entry #builder   a fresh `Builder` for this type, optionally sized
  entry #segment   the slice of a text over an `Interval` of ordinals, clamped to the text's bounds
      An interval reaching past the end is cut short, and one starting past the end is empty,
      rather than either failing.
  entry #zero   the empty value, as the `Zeroic` typeclass names it

entry .Builder   a mutable accumulator which builds a textual value from appended pieces
  detail
      `Builder[textual]` is the abstract class behind `TextBuilder`, `AsciiBuilder`,
      `WritingBuilder` and the builders of other textual types, each obtained from its
      `Textual#builder`. Pieces are added with `#append` (a value of the type, or a single
      `Char`), the result is read with `#apply`, and `#build` runs a block in which the
      package-level `append` and `appendln` write to it as a contextual value.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val builder = TextBuilder()
      builder.append(t"Hello").append(',').append(t" world")
      builder.length   // 12
      builder()        // t"Hello, world"
      ```

      A builder is mutable state and is not safe to share between threads; for text, prefer
      `Text.build`, which scopes one to a block.
  refer gossamer.TextBuilder
  refer gossamer.append
  refer gossamer.build
  entry #append   adds a value of the textual type, or a single `Char`; returns the builder
  entry #build   runs a block with this builder as the contextual `builder`, then returns the result
      Inside the block, `append(value)`, `appendln(value)` and `builder` refer to this
      builder. The block cannot outlive the call, which is why the builder is handed in as a
      pure contextual value despite being mutable.
  entry #apply   the accumulated value
  entry #clear   discards everything appended so far; returns the builder
  entry #nil   whether nothing has been appended
  entry #length   the number of elements appended so far

entry .TextBuilder   the `Builder` for `Text`, over a `StringBuilder`
  detail
      `TextBuilder(size)` sizes the underlying buffer, as a hint, when the length is known.
      `Textual.text.builder` returns one, and `Text.build` scopes one to a block.
  entry #put   appends one character
  entry #length   the number of characters appended so far

entry .AsciiBuilder   the `Builder` for `Ascii`, accumulating bytes
  detail
      Each appended `Char` is truncated to its low byte; an appended `Ascii` contributes its
      bytes. `Ascii`'s `Textual` instance uses it for every building operation.
  entry #put   appends one character as a byte
  entry #length   the number of bytes appended so far

entry .WritingBuilder   the `Builder` for `Writing`
  detail
      Accumulates the text of each appended `Writing` and recomputes the grapheme boundaries
      of the whole when the result is read, so `#length` counts characters while building,
      not graphemes.
  entry #length   the number of characters appended so far

entry .append   appends a value to the contextual `Builder`, inside a `build` block
    A package-level inline method for use inside `Text.build` or `Builder#build`, where a
    `Builder[textual]` is in scope as the contextual value `builder`. A `Text` is appended as
    is (converted to the builder's type), a `Char` as one element, and anything else through
    the builder's `Textual#Show` typeclass — `Showable`, for `Text` — so a value with no
    rendering is a compile error rather than a `toString`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    Text.build:
      append(t"x = ")
      append(42)
      append('!')
    // t"x = 42!"
    ```

entry .appendln   appends a value and then a newline to the contextual `Builder`
    As `append`, followed by `'\n'`.

entry .builder   the contextual `Builder` inside a `build` block, as a value
    `builder[TextBuilder]` (or `builder[Builder[Text]]`) retrieves the builder a `Text.build`
    or `Builder#build` block is writing to, for the operations `append` does not cover, such
    as `clear()` or reading `length`.

entry .build   `Text.build` scopes a `TextBuilder` to a block; `Array.build` fills a fresh array
    Two extensions share the name. `Text.build { … }` runs the block with a new `TextBuilder`
    as its contextual builder — `append`, `appendln` and `builder` inside it refer to that
    builder — and returns the text built, so a text assembled from many pieces is written
    without a visible mutable value:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    val table: Text = Text.build:
      for row <- 1 to 3 do
        append(t"row ")
        appendln(row)
    ```

    `Array.build[element](size) { array => … }` allocates a mutable JVM array of `size`
    elements, hands it to the lambda to fill, and returns it frozen as `Array[element]^{}`;
    `Pue.apply` and `Ascii`'s concatenation use it. The lambda's array must not escape.

entry .fill   `Text.fill(length)(index => char)`: a text of a given length, one character per index
    The index is zero-based (`Int`), so `Text.fill(3)(_ => '-')` is `t"---"` and
    `Text.fill(3)(i => ('a' + i).toChar)` is `t"abc"`.

entry .ascii   `Text.ascii(bytes)` and `bytes.ascii`: decodes a `Data` as US-ASCII text
    Both forms decode each byte as one ASCII character; a byte above 127 becomes the
    replacement character. For UTF-8, use `utf8`; for an arbitrary charset, decode with
    `data.as[Text]` under a `Charset` given (`textDecodable`).

entry .utf8   decodes a `Data` as UTF-8 text
    `bytes.utf8`: the inverse of `text.in[Data]` under `codepages.utf8Codepage`. Malformed
    sequences become the replacement character rather than failing.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage

    t"Adélaïde".in[Data].utf8   // t"Adélaïde"
    ```

entry .utf16   decodes a `Data` as UTF-16 text, honouring a byte-order mark
    `bytes.utf16`: without a mark, the bytes are read big-endian.

entry .pue   renders a `Data` as text in which every byte is one printable character
    `bytes.pue`: the _printable Unicode encoding_, a reversible rendering of bytes as text in
    which each byte is one character, chosen so that none is a control character — a byte
    which would be a control (its low seven bits at most 0x20, or 0x7f) is shifted up by
    0x100, and every other byte is the character of its own value. `Pue(text)` reverses it.
    The result is for display and for carrying bytes through text-only channels; it is not
    a standard encoding.

entry .Pue   decodes printable-Unicode-encoded text back to its bytes
  detail
      `Pue(text)` is the inverse of `bytes.pue`: each character's low byte is one byte of the
      result, so a byte which `pue` shifted up by 0x100 to avoid a control character comes
      back down. Text which did not come from `pue` is reduced to its characters' low bytes
      without complaint.
  entry .apply   the bytes a printable-Unicode-encoded text encodes

entry .cut   divides a text at every occurrence of a delimiter, into a list proved to be non-empty
    `text.cut(delimiter)` splits at a `Text`, a `Char` or a `Regex` (an `r"…"` literal), for
    any textual type with a `Cuttable` instance for that delimiter. The result is
    `List[textual] { type Occupied = true }`: a cut always yields at least one piece — the
    text after the last delimiter, or the whole text when the delimiter never occurs — so
    `head` and `last` on it are total, and `case List(a, b) =>` binds the element type.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"one,two,three".cut(t",")        // List(t"one", t"two", t"three")
    t",one,two".cut(',')              // List(t"", t"one", t"two")
    t"a1b22c".cut(r"[0-9]+")          // List(t"a", t"b", t"c")
    t"k=v=w".cut(t"=", 2)             // List(t"k", t"v=w")
    t"no commas".cut(t",").head       // t"no commas"
    ```

    A positive `limit` caps the number of pieces, the last taking the remainder; a limit of
    zero or less means unlimited. Trailing empty pieces are kept, as `String.split` keeps them
    with a positive limit, so `t"a,".cut(t",")` is `List(t"a", t"")`. `words`, `lines`,
    `unkebab` and `unsnake` are cuts at a space, a newline, `-` and `_`.

entry .Cuttable   the typeclass behind `cut`: how a textual type is divided at a kind of delimiter
  detail
      `textual is Cuttable by delimiter` has one method, `#cut(text, delimiter, limit)`, and
      must return at least one piece. Instances exist for `Text` cut by `Text` (a scan for the
      literal delimiter, with no regular expression involved) and by `Regex`, for any
      `Textual` with a `Countable` instance cut by `Text` or by `Regex` (so styled text is cut
      through `segment` and keeps its styling), and for any type cuttable by `Text` cut by a
      `Char`. All are companion givens, in implicit scope.
  entry .textualText   any textual cut by a literal `Text`; and any `Text`-cuttable type cut by a `Char`
      Two givens share the name. The first serves any `Textual` with `Countable`, scanning
      with `Textual#indexOf` and slicing with `segment`; the second derives `Cuttable by Char`
      from a type's `Cuttable by Text` instance, so `cut(',')` works wherever `cut(t",")` does.
  entry .textualRegex   any textual cut at the matches of a `Regex`
      The pieces *between* matches, with the text after the last match as the final piece. A
      zero-width match at the cursor is not a cut, so the scan always advances. `Text` itself
      has the more direct `.textRegex`.
  entry .textText   `Text` cut by a literal `Text`, by a plain substring scan
      No regular expression is compiled, so a delimiter such as `.` or `|` needs no quoting,
      and the cut works under runtimes whose regex engine is unavailable.
  entry .textRegex   `Text` cut at the matches of a `Regex`, through `String.split`
      The `limit` is passed to `split` as given (after `cut` has replaced a non-positive limit
      with unlimited), so trailing empty pieces are kept.
  entry #cut   the pieces of a text divided at a delimiter, at most `limit` of them, never none

entry .words   a text cut at every space
    `text.words` is `text.cut(t" ")`: consecutive spaces yield empty pieces, and no other
    whitespace is a boundary. The result is non-empty.

entry .lines   a text cut at every newline
    `text.lines` is `text.cut(t"\n")`; a `\r` before the newline stays on its line.

entry .unkebab   the words of a kebab-case identifier: cut at every `-`
    `t"one-two-three".unkebab` is `List(t"one", t"two", t"three")`. The inverse of `kebab`.

entry .unsnake   the words of a snake-case identifier: cut at every `_`
    `t"one_two_three".unsnake` is `List(t"one", t"two", t"three")`. The inverse of `snake`.

entry .uncamel   the words of a camel-case or Pascal-case identifier, each lowercased
    Breaks before every uppercase letter after the first character, and lowercases each word:
    `t"oneTwoThree".uncamel` and `t"OneTwoThree".uncamel` are both `List(t"one", t"two",
    t"three")`. A run of capitals is one word per letter (`t"parseXML"` gives `parse`, `x`,
    `m`, `l`). `kebab`, `snake`, `dotted`, `spaced`, `camel` and `pascal` rejoin the words in
    another convention:

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"oneTwoThree".uncamel.kebab    // t"one-two-three"
    t"oneTwoThree".uncamel.snake    // t"one_two_three"
    t"oneTwoThree".uncamel.dotted   // t"one.two.three"
    t"one-two-three".unkebab.camel  // t"oneTwoThree"
    ```

entry .join   concatenates a collection of texts, with optional separators and brackets
    An extension on any `Traversable` collection — `List`, `Set`, `Chain`, `Sequence`, a
    frozen array, or a standard-library `Iterable` — whose elements are `Joinable`: texts of
    any textual type, `Message`s, or collections, which join by concatenation. The result
    type is the element type, so joining `List[Text]` gives `Text` and joining `List[Teletype]`
    gives `Teletype`, with the separators of that same type.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    List(t"one", t"two", t"three").join                      // t"onetwothree"
    List(t"one", t"two", t"three").join(t", ")               // t"one, two, three"
    List(t"one", t"two", t"three").join(t", ", t" and ")     // t"one, two and three"
    List(t"one", t"two").join(t"(", t", ", t")")             // t"(one, two)"
    List(t"a", t"b", t"c").join(t"[", t", ", t" and ", t"]") // t"[a, b and c]"
    ```

    The five forms are: no separator; a separator; left bracket, separator and right bracket;
    a separator and a distinct *penultimate* separator placed before the last element, for
    prose; and all four. Two elements with a penultimate separator give `t"a and b"`.

    When the elements are not themselves joinable — `List(1, 2, 3)` — the separator is an
    *element*, interleaved into a rebuilt collection of the receiver's shape:
    `List(1, 2, 3).join(0)` is `List(1, 0, 2, 0, 3)`, and `List(List(1), List(2)).join(List(0))`
    is `List(1, 0, 2)`. `join` is the counterpart of `mkString`, but never calls `toString`:
    a collection whose elements have no joining does not compile. A `Set` joins in its
    iteration order, which the caller accepts by joining it.

entry .Joinable   the typeclass behind `join`: how a collection of values becomes one value
  detail
      `textual is Joinable` has one method, `#join(elements)`, which concatenates an
      `Iterable` of values into one. Instances in the companion cover every `Textual` type
      (through its builder, sized to the total length), `Message`, and the native collection
      shapes `List`, `Chain`, `Sequence` (by concatenation) and `Set` (by union). The
      receiver side of `join` is `Joinable.Source` (anything `Traversable`), and what the
      result is — a joined element, or a rebuilt collection with separators interleaved — is
      decided by `Joinable.Assembly`.
  refer gossamer.join
  entry .textual   any `Textual` type is `Joinable`, through its `Builder`
  entry .message   `Message`s join by concatenation
  entry .list   `List[element]`s join into one `List`, by concatenation
  entry .chain   `Chain[element]`s join into one `Chain`
  entry .sequence   `Sequence[element]`s join into one `Sequence`
  entry .set   `Set[element]`s join into one `Set`, by union
  entry .Source   the receiver side of `join`: a collection traversed to its elements
    detail
        `self is Joinable.Source by element` yields the elements of a receiver as an
        iterator. The one instance, `.traversable`, serves any `Traversable` receiver, which
        is every native shape and any standard `Iterable`. One typeclass rather than one
        `join` extension per receiver, since same-named extensions with different clause
        shapes would make every receiver ambiguous.
    entry .traversable   any `Traversable` receiver is a `Source` of its elements
    entry #traverse   the receiver's elements, in order, as an iterator
  entry .Assembly   how `join` assembles a receiver's elements, and the type of its separators
    detail
        `self is Joinable.Assembly by element to result` decides what `join` returns.
        `#Part` is the type of the separators and brackets, and `#assemble` turns the stream
        of parts into the result. Two instances are tried in priority order: `.joinable`,
        when the elements are themselves `Joinable` (text, messages, collections), in which
        case the result is one of them and the separators are of the same type; and the
        fallback `Fallback#interleaving`, when they are not, in which case the separators are
        elements and the result is a rebuilt collection of the receiver's shape, through
        `Reshapable`. The choice is made by given priority, since for a `List[Text]` both
        readings would apply and overloading cannot rank them.
    entry .joinable   elements which are `Joinable` join into one of them, with separators of that type
        `List(t"a", t"b").join(t",")` is `t"a,b"`. The element may be widened to a joinable
        supertype, so a list of a `Text` subtype joins as `Text`.
    entry .Fallback   the lower-priority assembly: separators interleaved as elements
      detail
          The trait which `Joinable.Assembly`'s companion extends, so that `#interleaving` is
          found only when `.joinable` does not apply.
      entry #interleaving   elements which are not `Joinable` are rebuilt with the separators interleaved
          Needs `self is Reshapable by element to result`. `List(1, 2, 3).join(0)` is
          `List(1, 0, 2, 0, 3)`.
    entry #Part   the type of a separator or bracket: the joined type, or the element type
    entry #assemble   builds the result from the stream of elements and separators
  entry #join   one value from an `Iterable` of them

entry .pascal   words joined in Pascal case: each capitalised, no separator
    An extension on any `Traversable` of a textual type with `Char` elements:
    `List(t"one", t"two").pascal` is `t"OneTwo"`. Each word is lowercased and then
    capitalised, so `List(t"ONE", t"two").pascal` is also `t"OneTwo"`.

entry .camel   words joined in camel case: Pascal case with the first letter lowered
    `List(t"one", t"two", t"three").camel` is `t"oneTwoThree"`. `uncamel` is its inverse.

entry .snake   words joined with `_`
    `List(t"one", t"two").snake` is `t"one_two"`; the words are not recased. `unsnake` is its
    inverse.

entry .kebab   words joined with `-`
    `List(t"one", t"two").kebab` is `t"one-two"`; the words are not recased. `unkebab` is its
    inverse.

entry .dotted   words joined with `.`
    `List(t"one", t"two").dotted` is `t"one.two"`.

entry .spaced   words joined with a space
    `List(t"one", t"two").spaced` is `t"one two"`, the inverse of `words`.

entry .length   the number of elements in a textual value: characters, bytes or graphemes
    `t"hello".length` is `5`; `Writing(t"👨‍👩‍👧").length` is `1`, one grapheme. An inline
    extension on any `Textual` type, through `Textual#length`.

entry .plain   the plain `Text` of any textual value, with styling dropped
    `Textual#text` as an extension: `teletype.plain` is the styled text's characters alone,
    and `text.plain` is `text` itself. The width-measuring operations (`pad`, `fit`, `center`)
    measure the plain text.

entry .init   a textual value without its last element
    `t"Hello".init` is `t"Hell"`, and the empty text's `init` is empty. The same as
    `skip(1, Rtl)`; `tail` is the general collection operation for the other end.

entry .chars   the characters of a textual value as a frozen `Array[Char]`
    `t"Hello".chars.to[List]` is `List('H', 'e', 'l', 'l', 'o')`. The array is of the plain
    text's characters, whatever the textual type. `text` on a frozen `Array[Char]` is the
    inverse.

entry .text   a frozen `Array[Char]` as a `Text`; and the contents of a `StringBuilder` as a `Text`
    `chars.text` is the inverse of `chars`. On a `StringBuilder`, `builder.text` reads the
    accumulated text, as the companion of `add`.

entry .add   appends a `Text` or a `Char` to a `StringBuilder`
    For code which works with a raw `StringBuilder`: `builder.add(t"…")` and `builder.add('x')`
    append without a `String` conversion at the call site, and `builder.text` reads the
    result. `TextBuilder` and `Text.build` are the typed alternatives.

entry .slices   a textual value divided into pieces of a fixed size
    `t"abcdefg".slices(3)` is `List(t"abc", t"def", t"g")`: every piece but the last has
    exactly `size` elements, and the last has whatever remains. Works on any `Textual` type
    through `segment`, so a styled text's pieces keep their styling. The empty text gives one
    empty piece.

entry .punch   splits a textual value at an ordinal, dropping the element there
    `t"key=value".punch(Quart)` is `(t"key", t"value")`: everything before the ordinal, and
    everything after it, with the element at the ordinal itself removed. Compare `snip`,
    which splits between elements and keeps them all.

entry .justify   widens a text to a width by distributing extra spaces among its inter-word gaps
    The intent is full justification: the difference between `width` and the text's length
    is shared out as additional spaces between the words, so a line fills its column. Only
    the plain character count is considered, not display width. The implementation is marked
    as needing attention in the source and should not be relied on yet.

entry .contains   whether a textual value contains a substring, or a character
    `t"hello world".contains(t"ello")` and `t"hello".contains('e')` are `true`; every text
    contains the empty text. The substring is plain `Text`, so a styled text is searched by
    its characters. For a position rather than a yes or no, use `offsetOf`.

entry .starts   whether a textual value begins with a prefix
    `t"hello".starts(t"he")` is `true`. Compared on the plain text; `chomp` removes the
    prefix if present.

entry .ends   whether a textual value ends with a suffix
    `t"hello".ends(t"lo")` is `true`. Compared on the plain text; `chomp(suffix, Rtl)`
    removes it if present.

entry .chomp   removes a prefix (or, with `Rtl`, a suffix) from a textual value, if it is there
    `t"Sir Toby".chomp(t"Sir ")` is `t"Toby"`, and `t"file.txt".chomp(t".txt", Rtl)` is
    `t"file"`; a text without the affix is returned unchanged, so `chomp` is safe to apply
    without a `starts` or `ends` check first.

entry .offsetOf   the position of a substring's first (or, with `Rtl`, last) occurrence, as an `Optional` ordinal
    `t"hello world".offsetOf(t"o")` is the ordinal `Quin`, and `offsetOf(t"o", Rtl)` the
    ordinal of the last `o`; a substring which does not occur is `Unset`, never a sentinel.
    The result is an `Ordinal`, so it feeds `before`, `after`, `upto`, `from`, `punch` and
    `segment` directly, which is how a text is taken apart around a delimiter:

    ```scala fuel
    frame
      label Find the separator
      code
          import soundness.*

          val header = t"Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8"
          val colon = header.offsetOf(t":")              // an Optional[Ordinal], present
    frame
      label Take the name before it
      code
          import soundness.*

          val header = t"Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8"
          val name = header.offsetOf(t":").let(header.before(_))    // t"Content-Type"
    frame
      label And the value after it, trimmed
      code
          import soundness.*

          val header = t"Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8"
          val value = header.offsetOf(t":").let(header.after(_).trim)
          // t"text/html; charset=utf-8"
    frame
      label Then split the value at the first parameter
      code
          import soundness.*

          val header = t"Content-Type: text/html; charset=utf-8"
          val value = header.offsetOf(t":").let(header.after(_).trim)
          val media = value.let { v => v.offsetOf(t";").lay(v)(v.before(_)) }   // t"text/html"
    ##
    ```

    `offsetOf` finds a substring; `pinpoint` finds a character by predicate, and `search`
    finds the matches of a regular expression. `Textual#indexOf` is the primitive beneath
    it. An empty substring is found at `Prim` from the left and is `Unset` from the right.

entry .tally   the number of non-overlapping occurrences of a substring
    `t"banana".tally(t"an")` is `2`, and `t"aaaa".tally(t"aa")` is `2`, since each
    occurrence is counted from the end of the one before. The empty substring tallies `0`.
    This is the substring form; `count(predicate)` on a text — `t"banana".count(_ == 'a')`
    — is the collections' operation, which is why the substring form has its own name.

entry .search   the substrings of a textual value which a regular expression matches
    `t"a1b22c".search(r"[0-9]+")` is a `Chain` of `t"1"` and `t"22"`, each a slice of the
    receiver (so a styled text's matches keep their styling). With `overlap = true`, a match
    may begin inside the one before it. It needs a `Regex.Engine` in scope, which the `r"…"`
    literal's form supplies. For the pieces *between* matches, use `cut`.

entry .extract   scans a textual value with a partial function of regex patterns, collecting every match
    `text.extract { case r"…" => value }` runs a partial function of `r"…"` patterns along
    the text, from `start` (`Prim` by default), yielding a `Chain` of the values the matching
    cases produce; the scan advances to the end of each match. The cases are decomposed while
    the code compiles so that each pattern is tried at every position, with a `Scanner` which
    records where its match began and ended; a lambda which cannot be decomposed falls back
    to re-running the whole partial function at each position. The result type is inferred
    from the cases.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    t"x=1, y=22".extract:
      case r"$key([a-z])=$value([0-9]+)" => (key, value)
    ```

entry .lower   a textual value in lower case
    `t"Hello World".lower` is `t"hello world"`, mapping each character with `Char#toLower`.
    Requires `Char` elements, so it is available on `Text`, `Writing` is excluded, and a
    styled text keeps its styling.

entry .upper   a textual value in upper case
    `t"Hello World".upper` is `t"HELLO WORLD"`, mapping each character with `Char#toUpper`;
    a character with no single-character upper case (`ß`) is left alone.

entry .capitalize   a textual value with its first character in upper case
    `t"hello".capitalize` is `t"Hello"` and `t"kingLear".capitalize` is `t"KingLear"`; the rest
    is unchanged. The empty text stays empty.

entry .uncapitalize   a textual value with its first character in lower case
    `t"Hello".uncapitalize` is `t"hello"`; `camel` is `pascal` followed by this.

entry .trim   a textual value without its leading and trailing whitespace, or one side's with a `Bidi`
    `t"  Hello \n".trim` is `t"Hello"`, and `trim(Ltr)` or `trim(Rtl)` strips one side only.
    Whitespace is what `Char#isWhitespace` says: spaces, tabs, newlines and the other
    Unicode space separators. Text which is all whitespace trims to the empty text.

entry .blank   whether a textual value is empty or all whitespace
    `t"  \t".blank` and `t"".blank` are `true`; for emptiness alone, use the collections'
    `nil`.

entry .pinpoint   the ordinal of the first character satisfying a predicate, from a start, in either direction
    `t"oh, Hello World".pinpoint(_.isUpper)` is `Quin`, the position of `H`;
    `pinpoint(_.isUpper, Sen)` begins the search at the sixth character and finds `W`; and
    `pinpoint(!_.isWhitespace, bidi = Rtl)` scans from the end. The result is `Unset` when no
    character satisfies the predicate. It is the positional primitive beneath `trim`, `blank`
    and `uncamel`, and reaches each character by direct access rather than traversal. For a
    substring's position, use `offsetOf`; the collections' `where` is the predicate search
    without a start or direction.

entry .tr   maps every character; or replaces one character with another
    `t"ABC".tr(_.toLower)` is `t"abc"`, and `t"one,two".tr(',', ';')` is `t"one;two"`. A
    character-for-character transliteration which never changes the length; for a
    substring, use `sub`.

entry .erase   a textual value with every occurrence of the given characters removed
    `t"a-b_c".erase('-', '_')` is `t"abc"`.

entry .broken   inserts a break character wherever two adjacent characters satisfy a predicate
    `text.broken((left, right) => …)` tests each adjacent pair and inserts `break` — a
    zero-width space by default — between the two when the predicate holds, so a long
    identifier can be given soft break points for wrapping: `t"camelCaseName".broken((a, b)
    => a.isLower && b.isUpper)` marks each word boundary. A text of fewer than two
    characters is unchanged.

entry .pad   extends a textual value with a character to a width, on the right or, with `Rtl`, the left
    `t"123".pad(5)` is `t"123  "` and `t"123".pad(5, Rtl, '0')` is `t"00123"`; text already
    at or past the width is unchanged, never truncated. The width is measured through the
    `Text is Measurable` given in scope (`textMetrics.uniformMetric` counts one column per
    character; `wideCharacterWidthMetric` counts two for CJK and emoji), so padding aligns
    columns of text as a terminal displays them.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import textMetrics.uniformMetric

    t"123".pad(5, Rtl)   // t"  123"
    ```

entry .center   pads a textual value on both sides to a width
    `t"ab".center(6)` is `t"  ab  "`, with the odd column, when there is one, on the left.
    Measured through the `Text is Measurable` given in scope, as `pad` is.

entry .fit   forces a textual value to an exact width, padding or truncating as needed
    `t"123".fit(5)` is `t"123  "`, `t"12345".fit(3)` is `t"123"`, and with `Rtl` the padding
    goes on the left and the truncation keeps the right: `t"12345".fit(3, Rtl)` is `t"345"`
    and `t"123".fit(5, Rtl, '.')` is `t"..123"`. Measured through the `Text is Measurable`
    given in scope.

entry .ossify   replaces every space with a non-breaking space
    `t"a b".ossify` has U+00A0 between the letters, so a line-wrapping layout keeps the
    words together.

entry .subscripts   a textual value with every character replaced by its subscript form
    `t"2".subscripts` is `t"₂"`; a character with no Unicode subscript becomes a space.

entry .superscripts   a textual value with every character replaced by its superscript form
    `t"23".superscripts` is `t"²³"`; a character with no Unicode superscript becomes a space.

entry .sub   replaces occurrences of a substring, or of a regular expression's matches, in a `Text`
    `t"naive".sub(t"i", t"ï")` is `t"naïve"`, and every occurrence is replaced unless a
    `count` is given: `t"aaa".sub(t"a", t"b", 2)` is `t"bba"`. A literal replacement is
    inserted verbatim — `\` and `$` in it are not escapes — so `t"a.b".sub(t".", t"\\.")`
    gives `a\.b`. With a `Regex` as the pattern, `$1` in the replacement refers to the
    first group: `t"ab".sub(r"(a)b", t"$$1c")` is `t"ac"`. Matches do not overlap:
    `t"fofofoo".sub(t"fofo", t"momo")` is `t"momofoo"`.

entry .urlEncode   a `Text` percent-encoded as a URL query component, in UTF-8
    `t"hello world".urlEncode` is `t"hello+world"` and `t"é".urlEncode` is `t"%C3%A9"`: the
    form encoding, in which a space is `+`. `urlDecode` reverses it.

entry .urlDecode   decodes a percent-encoded `Text`, with `+` as a space
    `t"%2B".urlDecode` is `t"+"` and `t"+".urlDecode` is `t" "`.

entry .punycode   an internationalised domain name in its ASCII (Punycode) form
    `t"www.äpfel.com".punycode` is `t"www.xn--pfel-koa.com"`, through `java.net.IDN`.

entry .sysData   the bytes of a `Text` in the system codepage, as a frozen array
    `t"hello".sysData` is the bytes `104, 101, 108, 108, 111`. For a chosen encoding, use
    `text.in[Data]` with a `codepages` given in scope.

entry .proximity   the distance between two `Text`s under the `Proximity` measure in scope
    `t"Hello world".proximity(t"Hello orld")` is `1` under `proximities.levenshteinProximity`
    (and `caseSensitivity.caseSensitive`): the result type is the measure's `Operand` — an
    `Int` for Levenshtein and prefix measures, a `Double` for Jaro, Jaro–Winkler and
    normalised Levenshtein. Both a `Proximity` and a `CaseSensitivity` must be imported by
    name.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import proximities.levenshteinProximity
    import caseSensitivity.caseSensitive

    t"kitten".proximity(t"sitting")   // 3
    ```

entry .fuzzy   matches a `Text` against literal cases by proximity rather than equality
    `text.fuzzy() { case t"hello" => … }` evaluates the case whose literal is nearest the
    text under the `Proximity by Double` measure in scope — `proximities.normalized
    LevenshteinProximity` or `jaroWinklerProximity` — with ties going to the earlier case. A
    `threshold` bounds the distance a case may be chosen at; a text further than it from
    every literal falls to the wildcard case, which must be last and may bind the text, and
    throws `MatchError` if there is none. Every non-wildcard case must be a `"…"` or `t"…"`
    literal, checked as the code compiles.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import proximities.normalizedLevenshteinProximity
    import caseSensitivity.caseInsensitive

    t"hlelo".fuzzy(0.5):
      case t"hello"   => t"english"
      case t"bonjour" => t"french"
      case other      => t"unknown: $other"
    // t"english"
    ```

entry .Proximity   a measure of the distance between two `Text`s: the typeclass behind `proximity`, `fuzzy` and `Lexicon`
  detail
      `Proximity by operand` has one method, `#distance(left, right)`, whose result type is
      `#Operand`: an `Int` for a count of edits or matching characters, a `Double` for a
      normalised score. There is no default: a measure is imported by name from the
      `proximities` package, and every one of them consults the `CaseSensitivity` in scope
      to decide whether two characters are the same, so that is imported too.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import proximities.levenshteinProximity
      import caseSensitivity.caseInsensitive

      t"Hello".proximity(t"hello")   // 0
      ```

      `#Triangulable` is `true` for a measure which is a metric obeying the triangle
      inequality (Levenshtein), which is what a `Lexicon` needs to prune its search; the
      Jaro measures are similarities, not metrics, and `Lexicon(terms)` will not accept
      them.
  refer gossamer.proximities
  refer gossamer.proximity
  refer gossamer.fuzzy
  refer gossamer.Lexicon
  entry #Operand   the type a distance has: `Int` or `Double`
  entry #Triangulable   `true` when the measure is a metric obeying the triangle inequality
  entry #distance   the distance between two texts

entry .proximities   the string-similarity measures, each a `Proximity` given to import by name
  detail
      Which notion of closeness `proximity`, `fuzzy` and `Lexicon` use is a choice, so
      `import soundness.*` brings none of these in; one is imported by name, along with a
      `CaseSensitivity`, which every measure needs to compare characters.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import proximities.jaroWinklerProximity
      import caseSensitivity.caseSensitive

      t"martha".proximity(t"marhta")   // about 0.96
      ```

      Levenshtein and prefix distances are `Int`s, and zero means identical; the Jaro,
      Jaro–Winkler and normalised Levenshtein measures are `Double`s. Jaro and Jaro–Winkler
      are *similarities* (one means identical), where normalised Levenshtein is a *distance*
      (zero means identical); `fuzzy`, which picks the case with the smallest value, expects
      a distance.
  entry .levenshteinProximity   the Levenshtein edit distance: single-character insertions, deletions and substitutions
      `t"kitten".proximity(t"sitting")` is `3`. The distance between a text and the empty
      text is the text's length. A true metric (`Triangulable = true`), so it is what a
      `Lexicon` is built over. Computed in two rows of integers, so memory is linear in the
      shorter text.
  entry .normalizedLevenshteinProximity   the Levenshtein distance divided by the longer text's length, in `0.0` to `1.0`
      `0.0` for identical texts, `1.0` for texts with nothing in common, and `0.0` for two
      empty texts. The measure `fuzzy` is usually used with, since a threshold on it does not
      depend on the texts' lengths.
  entry .jaroProximity   the Jaro similarity: matching characters within a window, less transpositions
      `1.0` for identical texts, `0.0` for texts sharing no characters within half the
      longer length of each other. A similarity, not a distance.
  entry .jaroWinklerProximity   the Jaro similarity boosted for a common prefix of up to four characters
      Jaro, plus a tenth of the common-prefix length (capped at four) times what Jaro
      leaves short of `1.0`; it favours texts which begin alike, as misspellings often do.
  entry .prefixProximity   the length of the common prefix of two texts
      `t"interview".proximity(t"internal")` is `5`. A larger number means *more* alike; it
      is a building block of `jaroWinklerProximity` rather than a distance for `fuzzy`.

entry .CaseSensitivity   whether two characters count as the same: a policy every proximity measure consults
  detail
      A `CaseSensitivity` is a function from two `Char`s to whether they match. The three
      policies in the `caseSensitivity` package — `caseSensitive`, `caseInsensitive` and
      `smartCase` — are the usual choices, imported by name; a custom one is any lambda of
      the right shape.
  refer gossamer.caseSensitivity
  entry #compare   whether two characters match under this policy

entry .caseSensitivity   the case-matching policies, each a `CaseSensitivity` given to import by name
  detail
      `import soundness.*` brings none of these in. One is imported by name wherever a
      `Proximity` is used, since every measure asks it whether two characters are the same.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Case-sensitive
        code
            locally:
              import proximities.levenshteinProximity
              import caseSensitivity.caseSensitive
              t"Hello".proximity(t"hello")   // 1: `H` and `h` differ
      alternative Case-insensitive
        code
            locally:
              import proximities.levenshteinProximity
              import caseSensitivity.caseInsensitive
              t"Hello".proximity(t"hello")   // 0: they are the same
      alternative Smart case
        code
            locally:
              import proximities.levenshteinProximity
              import caseSensitivity.smartCase
              (t"hello".proximity(t"Hello"), t"Hello".proximity(t"hello"))   // (0, 1)
      ##
      ```
  entry .caseSensitive   characters match only when they are equal
  entry .caseInsensitive   characters match when their upper-case forms are equal
  entry .smartCase   a lowercase character on the left matches either case; any other character matches only itself
      The rule of search tools such as Vim's `smartcase`: a query typed in lowercase is
      case-insensitive, and a capital in the query must be matched exactly. The left operand
      is the query.

entry .Collation   a total ordering of `Text`: what sorting and comparing text requires
  detail
      Text has no natural order — whether `café` sorts before `caff` depends on the language
      and the purpose — so Soundness has no ambient one: sorting a `List[Text]`, or writing
      `t"a" < t"b"`, needs a `Collation` in scope, and does not compile without one. A
      collation is imported by name from the `collations` package (`unicodeCollation` for
      dictionary order, `codepointCollation` for a fast, table-free order) or built from a
      hieroglyph `CollationTable` with `.apply`, and `collationComparable` turns it into the
      `Text is Comparable` the comparison operators and sorts look for.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import collations.unicodeCollation
      import sortingAlgorithms.timsort

      List(t"caff", t"café", t"cafe").sort   // List(t"cafe", t"café", t"caff")
      t"apple" < t"banana"                    // true
      ```

      `#compare` gives the `Comparison` of two texts, and `#key` a sort key: an array of
      integers which, compared element by element, orders as the collation does, for
      sorting many texts without repeating the comparison work.
  refer gossamer.collations
  refer gossamer.collationComparable
  entry .apply   a `Collation` over a hieroglyph `CollationTable`, such as a language's tailoring
  entry #compare   the `Comparison` (`Less`, `Same` or `More`) of two texts
  entry #key   a sort key for a text: integers which compare as the collation does

entry .collationComparable   makes `Text` `Comparable` whenever a `Collation` is in scope
    The given which `<`, `>`, `least`, `sort` and every other comparison on `Text` resolve
    through. It needs a `Collation` in scope, so text becomes sortable exactly when a sort
    order has been chosen, and the error for an unsorted `List[Text]` names the missing
    `Comparable`. It is a top-level given with a library-qualified name, since neither
    `Comparable`'s nor `Text`'s companion can refer to gossamer, so it must be imported by
    name — `import soundness.collationComparable` — alongside the collation.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.collationComparable
    import collations.codepointCollation

    t"b" < t"a"   // false
    ```

entry .collations   the sort orders for text, each a `Collation` given to import by name
  detail
      `import soundness.*` brings none of these in; a sort order is chosen by importing one.
      The two orders differ on case, accents and characters outside the basic plane:

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Unicode
        code
            locally:
              import collations.unicodeCollation
              import sortingAlgorithms.timsort
              List(t"B", t"a", t"café", t"cafe").sort   // List(t"a", t"B", t"cafe", t"café")
      alternative Codepoint
        code
            locally:
              import collations.codepointCollation
              import sortingAlgorithms.timsort
              List(t"B", t"a", t"café", t"cafe").sort   // List(t"B", t"a", t"cafe", t"café")
      ##
      ```

      A locale-specific tailoring comes from cosmopolite, which derives a `Collation` from a
      contextual `Locale`, or from `Collation(table)` over a hieroglyph `CollationTable`.
  entry .unicodeCollation   dictionary order: the Unicode Collation Algorithm's root table, three levels, non-ignorable
      The order of the Default Unicode Collation Element Table (UTS #10): letters by their
      primary weight regardless of case or accent, then accents, then case, so `cafe` <
      `café` < `caff` and `a` < `B`. The sort key is the table's key for the text.
  entry .codepointCollation   raw code-point order: deterministic, table-free, for machine-facing sorting
      Orders by Unicode scalar value, so it is stable across versions of the collation data
      and costs nothing to load; suited to file listings and other output which must be
      reproducible rather than readable. Not `String`'s order, which compares UTF-16 code
      units and so puts supplementary characters (`𝓐`) before U+E000–U+FFFF; here they sort
      after the whole basic plane. Capitals sort before every lowercase letter.

entry .Decimalizer   renders a `Double` as decimal text to a chosen precision, as the `DecimalConverter` `show` consults
  detail
      A `Decimalizer` is a `DecimalConverter`, the typeclass `Double is Showable` (and so
      `t"$x"` for a `Double`) looks for, which states how many digits are worth showing:
      `Decimalizer(3)` is three significant figures, and `Decimalizer(decimalPlaces = 2)` two
      digits after the point. Values whose magnitude reaches `#exponentThreshold` powers of
      ten (three by default) are written in scientific notation with a superscript exponent.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Decimalizer(3).decimalize(-3.14159)                  // t"-3.14"
      Decimalizer(5).decimalize(6.0221408e23)              // t"6.0221×10²³"
      Decimalizer(decimalPlaces = 1).decimalize(9.96)      // t"10.0"
      Decimalizer(2).decimalize(9.99)                      // t"10"
      Decimalizer(4).decimalize(999.96)                    // t"1.000×10³"
      Decimalizer(7, exponentMultiple = 3).decimalize(6.0221408e23)   // t"602.2141×10²¹"
      ```

      A `given Decimalizer(3)` in scope then makes every `show` of a floating-point number
      render the same way, so changing a program's precision is one given rather than a
      search for format strings. Rounding carries correctly past the leading digit. The
      special values render as `#infinity` (`∞`, with `#minusSign` when negative) and `#nan`
      (`∉ℝ`). `decimalConverters.javaDecimalConverter` is the plain `Double#toString`
      alternative.
  refer rudiments.DecimalConverter
  refer gossamer.decimalConverters
  entry #significantFigures   the number of significant digits to show, or `Unset` to use `#decimalPlaces`
      The first parameter, so `Decimalizer(3)` sets it. When both it and `#decimalPlaces`
      are `Unset`, three significant figures are used.
  entry #decimalPlaces   the number of digits after the decimal point, used when `#significantFigures` is `Unset`
  entry #decimalPoint   the decimal point character, `.` by default
      `Decimalizer(7, decimalPoint = '·').decimalize(6.626070e-34)` is `t"6·626070×10¯³⁴"`.
  entry #minusSign   the character written before a negative number, `-` by default
  entry #plusSign   the character written before a positive number, or `Unset` (the default) for none
  entry #exponent   the text between the mantissa and the exponent, `×10` by default
  entry #exponentThreshold   the magnitude, in powers of ten, at which scientific notation begins; `3` by default
      `Unset` disables scientific notation altogether.
  entry #superscript   whether the exponent is written in superscript digits (`10²³`), the default, or plain (`10-34`)
      With superscript, a negative exponent uses the macron-like `¯` as its sign.
  entry #exponentMultiple   rounds the exponent down to a multiple, `3` for engineering notation; `1` by default
      With `exponentMultiple = 3`, `6.0221408e23` renders as `602.2141×10²¹`.
  entry #infinity   the text for an infinite value, `∞` by default
  entry #nan   the text for a value which is not a number, `∉ℝ` by default
  entry #decimalize   the text of a `Double` under this decimalizer's settings
  entry #exponentScale   the number of decimal digits in an exponent; a helper of `#decimalize`

entry .decimalConverters   the `DecimalConverter` alternatives to a `Decimalizer`, to import by name
  detail
      A `Double` is shown through the `DecimalConverter` in scope. A `Decimalizer` states a
      precision; this package holds the converter which does not.
  entry .javaDecimalConverter   renders a `Double` with `Double#toString`, exactly as Java would
      `3.14159` renders as `3.14159`, `1e21` as `1.0E21`, and `0.1 + 0.2` as
      `0.30000000000000004`: every digit the binary value has, with no rounding to a
      precision.

entry .Numerous   a word with its singular and plural endings, for counting things in prose
  detail
      `Numerous(t"item")(3)` is `t"items"` and `Numerous(t"item")(1)` is `t"item"`. The
      endings are `s` and nothing by default, and are given for irregular words:
      `Numerous(t"quer", t"ies", t"y")`. A collection may be passed in place of a count.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val files = Numerous(t"file")
      def report(n: Int): Text = t"$n ${files(n)} changed"
      report(1)   // t"1 file changed"
      report(2)   // t"2 files changed"
      ```
  entry #word   the stem the endings are added to
  entry #pluralEnd   the ending for any count but one; `s` by default
  entry #singularEnd   the ending for a count of one; empty by default
  entry #apply   the word with the ending for a count, or for a collection's size

entry .Range   the home of `Range.Error`, raised when an index is outside a text's bounds
  detail
      Only the error lives here. The slicing operations themselves clamp rather than fail —
      `segment` cuts an oversized interval short — so the error is raised by the few
      operations which must reject an index, such as a line editor's cursor movement.
  entry .Error   Error 151: _the index `index` is outside the range `from`-`to`_
    detail
        Raised when a position is outside a text's bounds by an operation which cannot
        clamp. It is a `fulminate.Error`, so it is raised through a `Tactic[Range.Error]`
        and can be handled with `safely` or `mend`.
    entry #index   the index which was asked for
    entry #from   the lowest valid index
    entry #to   the highest valid index

entry .Bidi   the direction an operation works from: `Ltr` (the start) or `Rtl` (the end)
    `Bidi` is anticipation's enum, re-exported here with its cases so that `import
    gossamer.*` has the whole slicing vocabulary. `keep`, `skip`, `pad`, `fit`, `trim`,
    `chomp`, `offsetOf` and `pinpoint` each take a `Bidi`, `Ltr` by default, and `Rtl` makes
    them work from the end: `t"Hello".keep(2, Rtl)` is `t"lo"`.

entry .Ltr   left-to-right: from the start of the text, the default direction
    `Bidi.Ltr`, re-exported so it can be written bare: `t"123".pad(5, Ltr, '.')` is
    `t"123.."`.

entry .Rtl   right-to-left: from the end of the text
    `Bidi.Rtl`, re-exported so it can be written bare: `t"123".pad(5, Rtl, '.')` is
    `t"..123"`, and `t"Hello".skip(1, Rtl)` is `t"Hell"`.

entry .reversibleTextual   a `Reversible` instance for any textual type, for a companion to publish
    `reverse` is one extension for collections and text alike, driven by rudiments'
    `Reversible` typeclass; but a blanket given for every `Textual` type cannot be placed
    where implicit search would find it, since `Reversible`'s companion is in `rudiments`,
    below gossamer. So each textual type publishes the instance in its own companion:

    ```scala fuel
    substitute ???  ...
    ##
    import soundness.*

    case class Styled(text: Text)

    object Styled:
      given textual: Styled is Textual = ???
      given reversible: (Styled is Reversible { type Result = Styled }) = reversibleTextual
    ```

    The refinement must be parenthesised as shown, or the braces parse as the given's body.
    The reversal builds the result element by element through the type's `Builder`, so a
    styled text stays styled.

entry .traversableTextual   a `Traversable` instance for any textual type, for a companion to publish
    The counterpart of `reversibleTextual` for traversal: a textual type whose companion
    declares `given traversable: (Styled is Traversable { type Operand = Char }) =
    traversableTextual` gains the predicate forms of `keep`, `skip`, `snip`, `count`,
    `where`, `all`, `exists` and the other `Traversable` operations from the collections
    library, with the rebuild going through `segment` so styling survives. The elements
    traversed are the type's `Textual#Result`.

entry .Writing   a text segmented into grapheme clusters: the characters a reader sees
  detail
      A `Char` is not what a person calls a character: `é` may be one code point or two, a
      flag is a pair of regional indicators, and a family emoji is several code points
      joined. `Writing(text)` computes the text's grapheme-cluster `#boundaries` (Unicode
      UAX #29) once, and then presents it as a `Textual` whose elements are `Grapheme`s, so
      `length`, `keep`, `skip`, `segment`, `slices` and `reverse` count and cut by what is
      seen rather than by UTF-16 code units.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Writing(t"abc").graphemeCount           // 3
      Writing(Text("👨‍👩‍👧")).graphemeCount     // 1
      Writing(Text("🇬🇧🇫🇷")).length           // 2
      Writing(t"").boundaries.length          // 1: the single boundary at position 0
      Writing(t"héllo").keep(2).text          // t"hé", whatever `é` is made of
      ```

      Under a `Grapheme is Measurable` in scope (from a `textMetrics` given), `.measurable`
      gives a writing's display width as the sum of its graphemes' widths, which is more
      accurate than measuring the plain text per code point. A `Writing` shows as its text
      and inspects with its clusters separated by `·`.
  refer gossamer.Grapheme
  refer gossamer.Textual
  entry .apply   a `Writing` of a text, with its grapheme boundaries computed
  entry .empty   the empty writing
  entry .showable   shows a writing as its plain text
  entry .inspectable   inspects a writing as `w"…"` with `·` between its graphemes
      `Writing(t"a\r\nb").inspect` is `w"a·\r\n·b"`: the segmentation is what distinguishes
      one writing from another, so it is what inspection shows.
  entry .concatenable   `left + right` on writings, with the boundaries recomputed
  entry .measurable   the display width of a writing: the sum of its graphemes' widths
      Needs a `Grapheme is Measurable` in scope, which `textMetrics.wideCharacterWidthMetric`
      provides through `Grapheme.measurable`. A combining accent adds nothing to its base,
      and a flag or a ZWJ sequence is one wide cell rather than the sum of its parts.
  entry .textual   the `Textual` instance for `Writing`, with `Grapheme` elements
      Length is the grapheme count, access and `segment` work in graphemes, and `indexOf`
      returns a position only when the substring begins on a grapheme boundary.
  entry .graphemes   the writing's graphemes, as an indexed sequence
  entry .graphemeCount   the number of grapheme clusters; the same as `length`
  entry #text   the underlying plain text
  entry #boundaries   the character offsets of the grapheme boundaries, from `0` to the text's length
      A frozen array with one more element than there are graphemes; grapheme `i` is the
      characters between `boundaries(i)` and `boundaries(i + 1)`.

entry .Grapheme   one grapheme cluster: a base character with its combining marks, or a joined sequence
  detail
      The element type of `Writing`: an opaque type over `String` holding the code points of
      a single user-perceived character. `Grapheme("é")` wraps one; `#text` is it as `Text`
      and `#chars` its length in UTF-16 units. It inspects as `‹é›`, the cluster shown
      intact inside guillemets rather than escaped, since the structure is the point.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.wideCharacterWidthMetric

      Grapheme("a").metrics    // 1
      Grapheme("é").metrics    // 1: a base plus a combining mark
      Grapheme("日").metrics   // 2
      ```

      Its companion's members — `apply`, the `showable`, `inspectable` and `measurable`
      givens, and the `text` and `chars` extensions — are declared in
      `internal.opaques.Grapheme`, from which the type is exported, and documented there.
  refer gossamer.Writing

entry .Ascii   a byte string of ASCII characters, made with `a"…"`
  detail
      An opaque type over a frozen `Array[Byte]`, one byte per character, with a `Textual`
      instance whose elements are `Byte`s, so the positional and substring operations
      (`cut`, `keep`, `starts`, `offsetOf`, `slices`, `+`, …) work on it directly and `plain`
      converts it to `Text`. The `a"…"` interpolator checks every character is ASCII as the
      code compiles; `Ascii(bytes)` wraps bytes already in hand, unchecked.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val greeting: Ascii = a"hello"
      greeting.bytes.length          // 5
      (greeting + a", world").plain  // t"hello, world"
      greeting.inspect               // t"ascii\"hello\""
      ```

      Its companion's members — `apply`, the `showable`, `inspectable`, `concatenable` and
      `textual` givens, and the `bytes` extension — are declared in `internal.opaques.Ascii`,
      from which the type is exported, and documented there.
  refer gossamer.a

entry .textDecodable   decodes a `Data` to `Text` through the `Charset` in scope, for `data.as[Text]`
    The `Decodable in Data` given for `Text`, in `gossamer.codec`: `bytes.as[Text]` reads
    the bytes in the `Charset` given in scope (`charsets.utf8Charset`, `asciiCharset`, …),
    and is the counterpart of `text.in[Data]` under a `codepages` given. A charset other
    than ISO-8859-1 also needs a `TextSanitizer` for malformed input (`strictSanitizer`
    raises `Charset.Error`). It is a top-level given and so must be imported by name:
    `import soundness.textDecodable`.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import soundness.textDecodable
    import charsets.utf8Charset
    import textSanitizers.skipSanitizer

    val bytes: Data = Data(0x41, 0x42)
    bytes.as[Text]   // t"AB"
    ```

entry .enumIdentification   how an enum's case names are written as text, each an `Identifiable` given to import by name
  detail
      A `Verbosity.Loud` case may appear in a command line, a configuration file or a JSON
      document as `loud`, `LOUD`, `Loud` or `loud-level`; which, is a choice, so the
      `Identifiable` givens for enums live here and one is imported by name. Each encodes a
      case's name into the convention and decodes text in that convention back to the
      Pascal-case name the enum declares.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import enumIdentification.kebabCaseIdentifiable

      enum Verbosity:
        case Quiet, Normal, VeryLoud

      summon[Verbosity is Identifiable].encode(t"VeryLoud")   // t"very-loud"
      summon[Verbosity is Identifiable].decode(t"very-loud")  // t"VeryLoud"
      ```

      A CLI flag typed `Flag[Verbosity]` then reads `--verbosity very-loud`, and a JSON or
      TEL codec writes the case as `very-loud`.

      `pascalCaseIdentifiable` is the identity, for an enum whose cases are written as
      declared. The givens are in `gossamer.codec`, since `Identifiable` is distillate's.
  entry .kebabCaseIdentifiable   `VeryLoud` ↔ `very-loud`
  entry .snakeCaseIdentifiable   `VeryLoud` ↔ `very_loud`
  entry .camelCaseIdentifiable   `VeryLoud` ↔ `veryLoud`
      Decoding treats `_` as a word boundary, as the snake-case given does, before
      recapitalising.
  entry .pascalCaseIdentifiable   `VeryLoud` ↔ `VeryLoud`: the case name as declared

entry .Lexicon   a BK-tree over a vocabulary: finds every term within a given distance of a query
  detail
      Comparing a query against every word in a vocabulary is quadratic, and a spelling
      suggestion needs it to be fast. A `Lexicon` indexes terms in a
      Burkhard–Keller tree under the `Proximity by Int` in scope, and uses the triangle
      inequality — which the measure must obey, so its `Triangulable` must be `true`, as
      `levenshteinProximity`'s is — to prune most of the vocabulary without measuring it.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import proximities.levenshteinProximity
      import caseSensitivity.caseSensitive

      val lexicon = Lexicon(List(t"book", t"boot", t"cook", t"look", t"bake"))
      lexicon.search(t"book", 0)   // Set(t"book"): exact matches only
      lexicon.search(t"booq", 1)   // Set(t"book", t"boot"): within one edit
      ```

      A lexicon may map each term to a value (`Lexicon(Map(t"colour" -> Colour.Blue))`),
      in which case a search returns the values. It is mutable: `#update` adds a term,
      `lexicon(t"term") = value`, and the structure is not safe to share between threads
      while being built.
  refer gossamer.Proximity
  refer gossamer.proximities
  entry .apply   a lexicon of a list of terms, of a map from terms to values, or an empty one
      `Lexicon(terms)` indexes each `Text` as both key and value; `Lexicon(map)` indexes
      the keys and returns the values from a search; and `Lexicon[element]()` is empty, to
      be filled with `#update`. The first two need a `Triangulable` measure; the empty form
      takes any `Proximity by Int`, and relies on the caller to have chosen a metric.
  entry .Node   one node of the tree: a term, its value, and children keyed by distance
    detail
        The implementation of a non-empty lexicon. Each child subtree is keyed by its root's
        distance from this node's term, which is what lets a search skip every subtree whose
        key is further than the radius from the query's distance.
    entry #children   the child subtrees, keyed by their distance from this node's term
    entry #update   adds a term to the subtree at its distance, creating one if needed
    entry #search   the values within `radius` of the query, in this subtree
  entry #update   adds a term with a value: `lexicon(key) = value`
  entry #search   the values of every term within `radius` of the query
      Radius zero is an exact lookup; each wider radius admits more distant candidates,
      which is how a "did you mean" suggestion is produced from a large word list.

entry .Dictionary   a dense trie from `Text` keys to values, for exact and prefix lookup
  detail
      A `Dictionary[value]` maps text to values through a trie laid out in flat arrays:
      each node owns a block of `alphabet.size` slots in `#children`, so a lookup costs one
      array read per character of the key, and no hashing of the whole key. It suits
      hyphenation tables, HTML attribute names, keyword sets and any other fixed vocabulary
      which is looked up constantly.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      val dictionary = Dictionary(t"color" -> 0, t"colour" -> 1)
      dictionary(t"color")    // 0
      dictionary(t"colo")     // Unset
      dictionary.size         // 2
      ```

      The alphabet — the characters keys may contain, each given a slot — is derived from
      the keys by `.apply` and `.empty`, or given to `.withAlphabet` and `.aho`; a key with a
      character outside the alphabet is silently dropped. `.aho` additionally builds the
      failure links of an Aho–Corasick automaton, so one pass over an input finds every key
      which ends at each position, through `#step`, `#fail` and `#dictLink`.

      A dictionary is immutable once built, and its arrays are frozen, so it may be shared
      freely; `#add`, `#+` and `#++` rebuild a new one with a widened value type. The value
      type should be a reference type, since values are stored boxed in an `AnyRef` array.
  refer gossamer.Lexicon
  entry .Alphabet   the characters a dictionary's keys may use, each mapped to a dense slot
    detail
        A fixed mapping from `Char` to a slot in `[0, size)`, with `-1` for a character
        outside it. `Alphabet.of(t"abc…")` numbers the characters of a string in order; the
        default builders derive one from the keys automatically, so most callers never make
        one. Only characters below 128 can be in an alphabet. An alphabet is pure and may be
        shared by every dictionary built over it.
    entry .of   an alphabet of the characters of a string, in that order; ASCII only
    entry .empty   the alphabet with no characters, used by the empty dictionary
    entry #slot   the slot of a character, or `-1` if it is not in the alphabet
    entry #char   the character at a slot
    entry #size   the number of characters, and so the width of each node's block of children
  entry .empty   the dictionary with no entries
      Its alphabet is empty; `+` and `++` rebuild it with an alphabet derived from the keys.
  entry .apply   a dictionary of `(key -> value)` pairs, with the alphabet derived from the keys
      The alphabet's slots follow the order the characters are first seen in, so it is
      stable for the same input.
  entry .withAlphabet   a dictionary of pairs over a given alphabet
      A key with a character outside the alphabet is dropped.
  entry .aho   a dictionary of pairs over an alphabet, with Aho–Corasick failure and dictionary-suffix links
      Fills `#depth`, `#fail` and `#dictLink`, which the plain builders leave empty, so that
      a walk over an input with `#step` can follow a failure link on a mismatch and report
      every key ending at the current position through `#dictLink`.
  entry #children   the child-node table: `children(node * alphabet.size + slot)` is the child, or `-1`
  entry #values   the value at each node, or `null` where no key ends; typed `AnyRef` because arrays are invariant
      Read a typed value with `#value(node)` instead.
  entry #depth   each node's depth, for an Aho–Corasick dictionary; empty otherwise
  entry #fail   each node's failure link, for an Aho–Corasick dictionary; empty otherwise
  entry #dictLink   each node's nearest ancestor on the failure chain which holds a value; empty unless built with `aho`
  entry #alphabet   the alphabet the keys are encoded in
  entry #root   the root node's id, `0`
  entry #step   the node reached from a node by one character, or `-1` if no key continues that way
      The primitive of every lookup: start at `#root` and step once per character.
  entry #value   the value at a node, or `null` where no key ends there
  entry #apply   the value for a key, or `Unset`; also a lookup of a slice of a character buffer
      `dictionary(t"key")` is the exact-key form. `dictionary(buffer, offset, length)` looks
      up the characters of a mutable `Array[Char]` slice without allocating a `Text`, for a
      parser which already holds a buffer.
  entry #size   the number of keys stored
  entry #iterator   the stored values, as an `Iterable`
  entry #entries   every `(key, value)` pair, in alphabet order
  entry #add   a new dictionary with one more entry, as `#+` with the key and value separate
  entry #+   a new dictionary with an entry added, rebuilt with an alphabet covering both
      `dictionary + (t"key" -> value)`. The value type widens to a common supertype.
  entry #++   a new dictionary with several entries added

entry .internal   the macro implementations behind `t"…"`, `txt"…"`, `a"…"`, `extract` and `fuzzy`, and the opaque types' home
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home and so
      that `Ascii` and `Grapheme` can be opaque. Both are exported from `.opaques` to the
      `gossamer` package.
  entry .t   the macro behind `t"…"`: escape-processes the parts, renders substitutions through `Showable`
  entry .txt   the macro behind `txt"…"`: as `.t`, then folds whitespace runs and blank lines
  entry .ascii   the macro behind `a"…"`: checks each literal character is ASCII, reporting at its position
  entry .extractMacro   the macro behind `extract`: decomposes the cases and scans the text with a `Scanner`
  entry .fuzzyMacro   the macro behind `fuzzy`: collects the literal patterns and picks the nearest at runtime
  entry .opaques   the declarations of the opaque types `Ascii` and `Grapheme`
    detail
        The types' pages under the `gossamer` package have the full account; their
        companions' members are documented here, where they are declared.
    entry .Ascii   a byte string of ASCII characters; see `gossamer.Ascii`
      detail
          The companion of the opaque type `Ascii`, exported with it to the `gossamer` package.
      entry .apply   wraps a `Data` as `Ascii`, without checking its bytes
      entry .showable   shows an `Ascii` as the text its bytes encode
      entry .inspectable   inspects an `Ascii` as `ascii"…"`, with the characters escaped as a text literal's
      entry .concatenable   `left + right` on `Ascii` values
      entry .bytes   the underlying bytes, as `Data`
      entry .textual   the `Textual` instance for `Ascii`, with `Byte` elements
          `apply(text)` encodes text in the system codepage, `segment` and `concat` copy bytes,
          and the builder is an `AsciiBuilder`.
    entry .Grapheme   one grapheme cluster; see `gossamer.Grapheme`
      detail
          The companion of the opaque type `Grapheme`, exported with it to the `gossamer` package.
      entry .apply   a grapheme from the string of its code points; no check is made that it is one cluster
      entry .showable   shows a grapheme as its text
      entry .inspectable   inspects a grapheme as `‹…›`, the cluster unescaped
      entry .measurable   the display width of a grapheme: the widest of its code points
          Needs a `Char is Measurable` in scope (a `textMetrics` given). The maximum rather than
          the sum, so a base with combining marks is as wide as its base, and a flag pair or a
          ZWJ family is one wide cell.
      entry .text   the grapheme's code points as a `Text`
      entry .chars   the number of UTF-16 code units in the grapheme
